-- The compiler, built from the committed assembly in `bootstrap/` by
-- `scripts/build.sh`.
--
-- Three commands. `check` compiles as far as the last stage that can reject a
-- program, and writes nothing. `build` compiles all the way and writes the
-- result to `-o`, in the format `-f` names, or else the one the extension of
-- `-o` names, or else an executable. Without `-o`, an executable goes to the
-- working directory, named after the entry file, and the other formats to
-- standard output (`openSink`). `doc` checks the modules it is given and
-- writes what each exports as JSON (`Turkey.Doc`), or with `--html DIR` as a
-- website (`Turkey.DocSite`). `check` and `build` each take `--dump
-- STAGE,...` to print what a stage produced, which is how a pass is read
-- while it is being written: `check` goes on as far as the latest stage asked
-- for and no further, and `build` prints them on its way.
--
-- Several files may be named, and each is answered in turn with no separator
-- between them: every dump marks its own beginning or end, so the
-- concatenation is unambiguous. That keeps a whole corpus to one process,
-- since starting this program is not free.

